-- title:
--   Local infinitesimal lifting for the fine family
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ and $\bar r$ are primes with $\bar r \neq r$, $N$ is a nonzero squarefree natural number divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$.
--
--   **Local base data.** $\mathcal O$ is a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero which is a domain, $\pi$ an irreducible element of it, the $\pi$-adic topology being complete, with residue ring of cardinality $r$ and $(r) = (\pi)$; $K_0$ is a field of characteristic zero that is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Next, $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ (written `Onr`) is a characteristic-zero domain over $\mathcal O$ together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to: completeness for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, maximality of that ideal, integrality of every element over $\mathcal O$ modulo it, the property that every monic polynomial of positive degree has a root modulo it, and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^{r}$ modulo it. Finally $v_{\det} : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (multiplicatively written) is a homomorphism characterised by `hvdet`: $v_{\det}(g) = n$ exactly when $\det g$ is a unit of $\mathcal O$ times $\pi^{n}$.
--
--   **Indefinite side and moduli.** $a,b \in \mathbb Q$ are such that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ is indefinite and ramified exactly at $r$ and $\bar r$, $\Lambda$ a maximal order in it. $f : \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with the point-assignment `pt` is a coarse moduli space for fake elliptic curves with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure over $\mathcal O$-algebras (`IsCoarseModuli`: isomorphism-invariance, compatibility with base change, bijectivity on algebraically closed fields, and the universal property). An integer $n \geq 3$ is prime to $r$, to $\bar r$ and to $N$; $f_M : M \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with `ptF` is a fine moduli space for such curves equipped with a full level-$n$ structure (`IsFineModuli`), and $\rho : G \to \operatorname{Aut} M$, $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ is a level-twist action (`IsLevelTwistAction`: the automorphisms are over the base, twisting a full level structure by $\chi(g)$ moves `ptF` by $\rho(g)$, and $\chi$ is a bijective labelling modulo $n$). The morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ is over $\mathcal O$, is invariant under $\rho$, and carries `ptF` to `pt` (hypotheses `hp`, `hρp`, `hp_pt`).
--
--   **Hecke and Atkin–Lehner structure on $\mathcal X$.** For each prime $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$, $g_\ell : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with `ptT ℓ` is a coarse moduli space for curves with an extra $\ell$-level subgroup (`IsCoarseModuliT`), and $d_0(\ell), d_1(\ell) : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ are the two degeneracy maps over $\mathcal O$, $d_0$ forgetting the extra level and $d_1$ matching the target of an $\ell$-level isogeny (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`, `hd₀`, `hd₁`). The endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ realise the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ on moduli points (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **Definite side.** $a_1,b_1 \in \mathbb Q$ give a definite quaternion algebra ramified exactly at $\bar r$, with $\Lambda_1$ maximal, $R_1$ an Eichler order of level $N$ contained in $\Lambda_1$, an element $n_1$ of the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ whose intersection order $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is Eichler of level $Nr$, an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra embedding $\iota_0$ into $M_2(K_0)$, and the place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ above $r$. The subgroup $\Gamma_t$ of units is characterised by `hΓt` as the group of units integral for $R_1$ at all places other than $v$. The families $s$, $s_f$ indexed by the primes $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$ satisfy `hs`, four clauses per $\ell$: the components of $s_f(\ell)$ away from $r$ are $s(\ell)\otimes 1$, its component at $r$ is $1$, the product of the diagonal idele of $\ell$ with $s_f(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in the level Hecke set `levelHeckeUSet` of $\Lambda_1$ and the intersection order at $\ell$ when $\ell \mid N$ and in the prime Hecke set of that order at $\ell$ otherwise, and $\operatorname{nrd} s(\ell) = \ell$; $\Gamma_{t,\ell}$ is the intersection of $\Gamma_t$ with its conjugate by $s(\ell)$ (`hΓtℓ`). The unit $\bar w$ has reduced norm $\bar r$ and normalises $\Gamma_t$ (`hwbar`).
--
--   **Coordinates on $\Lambda$.** All rational integers lie in $\Lambda$ (`hΛℤ`), and `coord` is an order coordinate for $\Lambda$ at $r$ (`IsOrderCoord`: additive, unital, multiplicative in the twisted Witt-vector sense, injective, $r$-adically dense, and compatible with reduced traces).
--
--   **Data over the residue ring $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi$.** $A_0$ is a fake elliptic curve over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi$, $X_0$ a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module at $r$ of height $4$, $\theta_0$ a system of formal coordinates of dimension $2$ along the unit section of $A_0$, and $A_0$ is a formal module via `coord`, $X_0$ and $\theta_0$ (`hA₀`, `hX₀`). Endomorphisms $e(\gamma), e'(\gamma)$ of $A_0$ over the base, indexed by $\Gamma_t$, with degrees $\deg \gamma$, satisfy the group of hypotheses `hE1`, `hE1mul`, `hE1sc`, `hE1'`: each pair $(e(\gamma),e'(\gamma))$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg\gamma}$ preserving the level structure; $e$ is multiplicative after multiplying by suitable powers of $r$; a scalar $\gamma = c$ gives $e(\gamma)$ equal to multiplication by $c$ up to powers of $r$; and conversely, if $e(\gamma)$ agrees up to powers of $r$ with multiplication by a positive integer then $\gamma$ is a rational scalar. The hypothesis `hE2` states that for every injective ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $\mathcal O_D$-action and of $\varpi$ on $X_0$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that each $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ admits an element $\varepsilon$ of that centraliser inducing $e(\gamma)$ through $\theta_0$ on nilpotent arguments and with $E_0(\varepsilon) = r^{k_\gamma}\, g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$. The hypothesis `hE3` provides, for every full level-$n$ structure on $A_0$, a labelling $\Gamma_t \to \Lambda$ describing the action of $e(\gamma)$ on that structure, multiplicative modulo $n$ and sending scalars to scalars modulo $n$. The hypothesis `hE4` states that for every fake elliptic curve $A$ over an algebraically closed field over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi$ which is a pullback of $A_0$, every level-preserving self-isogeny pair of $r$-power degree on $A$ comes, up to powers of $r$, from some $e(\gamma)$.
--
--   **The order $R_2$ and its action.** $R_2 \leq R_1$ is an order such that every element of $R_1$ lies in $R_2$ after multiplication by a power of $r$, and $\hat e : R_2 \to \operatorname{End}(A_0)$ is a family of endomorphisms over the base. The hypotheses `hE5a`, `hE5b1`, `hE5b2`, `hE5b3`, `hE5c`, `hE5d`, `hE5e` require: each $\hat e(x)$ respects the group law, commutes with the $\Lambda$-action and preserves the level structure; $\hat e(1) = \mathrm{id}$; $\hat e(xy) = \hat e(y)$ followed by $\hat e(x)$; $\hat e(m)$ is multiplication by $m$ for rational integers $m$; $\hat e(\bar x)$ followed by $\hat e(x)$ is multiplication by $\operatorname{nrd} x$; $\hat e(x)$ agrees with $e(\gamma)$ up to powers of $r$ when $x = r^{k}\gamma$; and a joint version of `hE2` producing one matrix $g$ that simultaneously linearises the $\Gamma_t$-action and the $R_2$-action through $\iota_0$.
--
--   **The special formal module and its moduli package.** $\iota : \mathbb Z_{r^2} \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is a ring homomorphism, $\Phi$ a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ which is special for the induced structure map and of height $4$, `MD` a moduli package at $r$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfying the Zariski sheaf condition, and $\eta$ assigns to every rigidified object over a $\pi$-nilpotent ring a point of `MD`. The hypothesis `hη` has three clauses: on admissible rigidified objects over Noetherian rings $\eta$ identifies two objects precisely when they are isomorphic; $\eta$ is compatible with base change; and every point of `MD` is, after passing to a distinguished Zariski cover, of the form $\eta(t)$ for an admissible $t$. The ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the action of $\Phi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ satisfies `hE₀`: it is injective and there is $m$ such that every integral matrix occurs as $r^{-m}E_0(\varepsilon)$ and $r^{m}E_0(\varepsilon)$ is always integral.
--
--   **Drinfeld's identification.** The family $e_D$ identifies, for every Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, the points of the functor $G_{\mathcal O}(\mathrm{MD})$ with pairs consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ and a Deligne datum in $\Omega_{K_0,\pi}(B)$. The hypotheses `hnatD`, `hbijD`, `hfstD`, `hGLD`, `hPiD`, `hPiexD` state that $e_D$ is natural in $B$, bijective, compatible with the unramified leg, turns the relation `IsActBy` for $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ into the twisted action `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` (Frobenius twist of the leg by $-v_{\det}(g)$ together with pullback of the Deligne datum by $g^{-1}$), turns a $\pi$-translate into the Frobenius twist of the leg by $1$ with unchanged datum, and that $\pi$-translates always exist.
--
--   The reduction $\kappa$ from $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi$ to $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ is compatible with the quotient maps (`hκ`), and $\beta_0$ is an isogeny from $\Phi$ to the base change of $X_0$ along $\kappa$ of height $4 n_0$ (`hβ₀`). $P_0$ is a full level-$n$ structure on $A_0$.
--
--   **Atkin–Lehner data in the special fibre.** $A_{0w}$ is a further fake elliptic curve over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\pi$, with $a_w, a_w'$ exhibiting it as the Atkin–Lehner quotient of $A_0$ at $\bar r$ (`hALw`), with $b_w, b_w'$ an isogeny pair of degree $r^{k_w}$ back to $A_0$ preserving the level (`hBSw`, `hBSwlev`), and `hJOINTw` asserts the joint linearisation of `hE2` type in which, for the same $g$, the composite $a_w$ followed by $b_w$ is induced by an element $\varepsilon_w$ of the centraliser with $E_0(\varepsilon_w) = r^{k_w'}\, g\,\iota_0(\bar w)\,g^{-1}$.
--
--   **The comparison map $\Xi$.** $\Xi$ assigns to every rigidified curve over a Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with leg $\psi$ a point of $G_{\mathcal O}(\mathrm{MD})$ over $B$. The hypotheses `hΞleg`, `hΞnat`, `hΞiso`, `hΞdef` require: over connected $B$ the leg of $\Xi$ is a Frobenius twist $\mathrm{Fr}^{k}\psi$ of the given leg; $\Xi$ is compatible with pullback of curves and rigidifications; $\Xi$ is unchanged under an isomorphism of curves whose rigidifications are matched up to powers of $r$; and, over connected $B$ in which $r$ is nilpotent, $\Xi$ is computed by rigid transport: there are $j \leq 1$ and an admissible rigidified object $t$ with underlying formal module the given one, transported from $\theta_0$ through $\kappa$ and $\beta_0$, such that $\Xi$ equals the point with leg $\mathrm{Fr}^{-j}\psi$ and underlying point $\eta(t)$.
--
--   **Equivariance and the four comparison clauses.** For a fixed $g_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$: `heqΓ` states that translating a rigidification by $e(\gamma)$ moves $\Xi$ by the action of $r^{c}\, g_0 \iota_0(\gamma) g_0^{-1}$ for some integer $c$; `heqW` is the corresponding statement for an Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ (when $\bar r$ is invertible) and the element $g_0 \iota_0(\bar w) g_0^{-1}$. The hypotheses `hK1`–`hK4` express that, for the leg twisted by $-v_{\det}(g_0)$, the pairs (curve, rigidification) whose image under $\Xi$ has the prescribed leg and is $g_0^{-1}$-twisted-equivalent to a given pair (leg, Deligne datum) exist (`hK1`), are unique up to an isomorphism matching the rigidifications up to powers of $r$ (`hK2`), are stable under base change along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps (`hK3`), and lift along a surjection $p : B \to B_0$ with square-zero-type kernel condition, the lift being compatible with the twisted-action clause for all Deligne data over $B_0$ (`hK4`). The hypotheses `hC0e`, `hC0u` give existence, over connected $B$, and uniqueness of the normalised transport of the level-$n$ structure $P_0$ along a rigidification.
--
--   **The family $\Theta_f$.** $\Theta_f$ assigns, for every Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$, to a triple consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$, a Deligne datum $P$ over $B$ and an element $g \in G$, a $B$-point of $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. It is natural in $B$ (`hΘnat`); by `hΘval`, whenever $(E,\varrho)$ has $\Xi$-leg equal to the $(-v_{\det}(g_0))$-twist of $\psi$, is $g_0^{-1}$-twisted-equivalent to $(\psi,P)$, and $P_n$ is the normalised transport of $P_0$, then $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g)$ is the moduli point of $(E,P_n)$ followed by $\rho(g^{-1})$; by `hΘvalc`, under the same two conditions $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g)$ followed by $p$ is the coarse moduli point of $E$.
--
--   **Final data and conclusion.** $B$ and $B_0$ are Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebras in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, $p_B : B \to B_0$ a surjective $\mathcal O$-algebra map such that the product of any two elements of its kernel vanishes; $x_0 = ((\psi_0, P_0'), g)$ is a point over $B_0$ of the product functor (an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi_0 : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B_0$, a Deligne datum $P_0'$ over $B_0$, and an element of $G$); $y$ is a $B$-point of $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ whose image under $p_B$ is $\Theta_f(x_0)$; and $\mathfrak p$ is a prime ideal of $B$.
--
--   The assertion is that there exist: an element $c \in B$ with $c \notin \mathfrak p$; an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ with $p_B \circ \psi = \psi_0$; witnesses that $\pi$ is nilpotent in $B[1/c]$ and in $B_0[1/p_B(c)]$; an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $p_c : B[1/c] \to B_0[1/p_B(c)]$ compatible with $p_B$, in the sense that the localisation map $B \to B[1/c]$ followed by $p_c$ equals $p_B$ followed by the localisation map $B_0 \to B_0[1/p_B(c)]$; and a Deligne datum $P_c$ over $B[1/c]$, such that both of the following hold: the image of $P_c$ under $p_c$ equals the image of $P_0'$ under $B_0 \to B_0[1/p_B(c)]$; and $\Theta_f$ evaluated over $B[1/c]$ at the triple consisting of $\psi$ followed by $B \to B[1/c]$, the datum $P_c$, and the element $g = x_0.2$ of $G$, equals the image of $y$ under the localisation map $B \to B[1/c]$.
--
--   This is the local (on the base) existence half of the infinitesimal lifting criterion for the family of $B$-points of the fine moduli space $M$ constructed from the Čerednik–Drinfel'd data: over a square-zero thickening the Deligne datum and the unramified leg of a given point lift after inverting a single element avoiding a prescribed prime. It feeds the height-normalised connected lifting statement [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.fineFamily_exists_lift_of_value_of_squareZero_heightNormalised_conn`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.fineFamily_exists_lift_of_value_of_squareZero_heightNormalised_conn), and thence the identification of the formal completion of the Shimura curve with the quotient of Drinfel'd's formal upper half plane.
